without the disease to live at least 5 years after diagnosis</strong>.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"dd43fb0bdef8d1ea\">>>", "published": "April 10, 2013", "siteName": "www.asbestos.com"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"daa430e4f3b0ba23\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nMesothelioma Survival Rates & Factors That Affect Patients\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"daa430e4f3b0ba23\">>>", "url": "https://www.mesothelioma.com/mesothelioma/prognosis/survival-rate/", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c0cb6bafdba071b3\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nFor instance, the 5-year survival rate for pleural mesothelioma patients who receive treatment is <strong>12%</strong>. This means 12 out of 100 people with pleural mesothelioma can expect to live for five years following diagnosis.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c0cb6bafdba071b3\">>>", "published": "May 13, 2010", "siteName": "www.mesothelioma.com"}]}, "isError": false, "timestamp": 1775737894190}}
{"type": "message", "id": "0cbd8804", "parentId": "c1874804", "timestamp": "2026-04-09T12:31:49.974Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Great! I now have:\n- Stomach cancer: 5-year survival ~36%\n- Brain cancer (overall): varies, but glioblastoma has 5-7% survival rate\n- Mesothelioma: 5-year survival ~12%\n\nNow let me think about the 5 deadliest cancers (excluding pancreatic cancer). Based on the survival rates I've gathered:\n\n1. Glioblastoma (brain cancer) - ~5-7% survival\n2. Mesothelioma - ~12% survival\n3. Liver cancer - ~21-22% survival\n4. Esophageal cancer - ~22% survival\n5. Lung cancer - ~28% survival\n\nWait, I need to verify if mesothelioma is included in the deadliest cancers. Let me also check for other cancers with very low survival rates.\n\nActually, I should also check if mesothelioma is a good choice for the \"deadliest\" list since it's relatively rare. Let me think about what makes a cancer \"deadly\" - it could be based on:\n1. Total number of deaths\n2. Low survival rate\n\nThe user asked for \"most deadly cancers\" which could mean either. Let me search for the top cancer killers in terms of total deaths to make sure I'm covering the right ones.\n\nActually, looking at the SEER data I fetched earlier, the deadliest cancers by number of deaths are:\n1. Lung and bronchus cancer: 124,730 deaths\n2. Pancreatic cancer: 51,980 deaths (but user said to exclude this)\n\nLet me search for more information on cancer death rates.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_6d20ee0134af4d87a8e54dc5", "name": "web_fetch", "arguments": {"url": "https://seer.cancer.gov/statfacts/html/stomach.html"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_4a4777737d1041389d2eb35a", "name": "web_fetch", "arguments": {"url": "https://seer.cancer.gov/statfacts/html/livibd.html"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "openrouter", "model": "z-ai/glm-5", "usage": {"input": 18950, "output": 727, "cacheRead