ancer (the most aggressive type). The overall brain cancer survival rate is around 36%, but glioblastoma specifically has 5-7% survival.\n\nI should use \"Brain and other nervous system cancer\" or \"Glioblastoma\" specifically. Let me check the SEER data for brain cancer survival rate.\n\nFrom my search results:\n- Brain and other nervous system cancer: 5-year survival varies (76.9% localized, but overall around 36% for brain cancers)\n- Glioblastoma specifically: 5.7% survival\n\nI think I should use glioblastoma since it's the deadliest brain cancer. But let me also consider mesothelioma - is it a cancer that should be on the \"deadliest\" list?\n\nMesothelioma is relatively rare (about 3,000 cases per year in the US), but has a very low survival rate (12%). \n\nLet me reconsider what the user means by \"deadly\". In oncology, \"most deadly\" typically refers to cancers that cause the most deaths, not necessarily those with the lowest survival rates. However, the user asked for survival rates in brackets, which suggests they want to know about mortality per diagnosis.\n\nActually, looking at the data I fetched, the table shows both new cases and deaths. The most deadly cancers by total deaths are:\n1. Lung cancer - 124,730 deaths\n2. Pancreatic cancer - 51,980 deaths (excluded)\n3. Colorectal cancer - 52,900 deaths\n4. Liver cancer - 30,090 deaths\n5. Stomach cancer - 10,780 deaths (but this seems low compared to others)\n\nWait, that doesn't look right. Let me check the order again from the SEER table I fetched:\n\nFrom the SEER table:\n1. Lung and Bronchus Cancer - 124,730 deaths\n2. Pancreatic Cancer - 51,980 deaths\n3. Colorectal Cancer - 52,900 deaths\n4. Liver cancer - 30,090 deaths\n5. (Need to find #5)\n\nActually, looking more carefully at the table from my liver cancer fetch:\n- Lung and Bronchus Cancer: 124,730 deaths\n- Pancreatic Cancer: 51,980 deaths\n- Colorectal Cancer: 52,900 deaths (wait, this is higher than pancreatic!)\n\nLet me re-examine. The table shows:\n1. Breast Cancer (Female): 42,170 deaths\n2. Prostate Cancer: 35,770 deaths\n3. Lung and Bronchus Cancer: 124,730 deaths\n4. Colorectal Cancer: 52,900 deaths\n5. Melanoma: 8,430 deaths\n...\n\nWait, that's ordered by new cases, not deaths. Let me look at the death numbers:\n- Lung: 124,730 deaths\n- Colorectal: 52,900 deaths\n- Pancreatic: 51,980 deaths\n- Breast: 42,170 deaths\n- Liver: 30,090 deaths\n- Prostate: 35,770 deaths\n\nHmm, prostate has 35,770 deaths which is more than liver's 30,090.\n\nSo by total deaths (excluding pancreatic):\n1. Lung and Bronchus Cancer: 124,730 deaths\n2. Colorectal Cancer: 52,900 deaths\n3. Breast Cancer: 42,170 deaths\n4. Prostate Cancer: 35,770 deaths\n5. Liver Cancer: 30,090 deaths\n\nBut this doesn't align with the 5-year survival rate approach. Let me think about what makes more sense...\n\nActually, I think the most sensible interpretation is that \"most deadly\" refers to the combination of high mortality and high death toll. The user wants survival rates, which suggests they want to understand how lethal each cancer is per diagnosis.\n\nLet me take a hybrid approach: I'll list the cancers that are both common causes of cancer death AND have low survival rates. This would give:\n1. Lung cancer - highest death toll, 28% survival\n2. Liver cancer - high death toll, 22% survival\n3. Esophageal cancer - moderate death toll, ~22% survival\n4. Stomach cancer - moderate death toll, 37.9% survival\n5. Brain cancer (glioblastoma) - moderate death toll, 5-7% survival for glioblastoma\n\nBut wait, stomach cancer has 37.9% survival, which is higher than some others. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually, I think the most straightforward interpretation is:\n- \"Deadly\" = low survival rate (high mortality per diagnosis)\n- The user wants to know which cancers are most lethal\n\nLet me go with the 5 cancers with the lowest survival rates (excluding pancreatic cancer):\n\n1. Glioblastoma/Brain cancer - 5-7% survival (for glioblastoma)\n2. Mesothelioma - 12% survival\n3. Liver cancer - 22% survival\n4. Esophageal cancer - 22% survival\n5. Lung cancer - 28% survival\n\nBut I'm concerned about glioblastoma and mesothelioma - they might be considered \"uncommon\" cancers and might overlap with my uncommon cancer list.\n\nLet me reconsider. The user wants:\n- 5 most deadly cancers\n- 5 uncommon cancers\n- No overlap between lists\n\nIf glioblastoma and mesothelioma are rare/uncommon, they should go in the uncommon list, not the deadly list.\n\nLet me think about this differently. The most deadly cancers by total deaths (excluding pancreatic) are:\n1. Lung cancer - 124,730 deaths, 28% survival\n2. Colorectal cancer - 52,900 deaths, ~65% survival\n3. Breast cancer - 42,170 deaths, ~91% survival\n4. Prostate cancer - 35,770 deaths, ~97% survival\n5. Liver cancer - 30,090 deaths, 22% survival\n\nBut breast and prostate have high survival rates, so they're not \"deadly\" in the sense of being lethal per diagnosis.\n\nI think the best approach is to define \"most deadly\" as cancers with the lowest survival rates among common cancers. Let me go with:\n\n1. Lung cancer - 28% survival, 124,730 deaths\n2. Liver cancer - 22% survival, 30,090 deaths\n3. Esophageal cancer - 22% survival\n4. Stomach cancer - 37.9% survival\n5. Brain cancer - varies, but overall ~36% for brain and CNS\n\nWait, but I found that glioblastoma has 5-7% survival. Let me check if brain cancer overall should be on this list or if glioblastoma (a subtype) is too rare.\n\nFrom my search results, glioblastoma accounts for 49% of malignant brain cancers. So brain cancer is a common cancer category, but glioblastoma is a subtype.\n\nLet me use \"Brain and other nervous system cancer\" with the overall survival rate, which I need to find.\n\nFrom my search results, I see that the 5-year survival for brain cancer varies. Let me search for the overall brain cancer survival rate.\n\nActually, from the SEER data I saw:\n- Localized brain cancer: 35.3% survival\n- Overall brain cancer survival: around 36%\n\nLet me verify this.\n\nActually, looking at my previous search results, I saw:\n\"Brain cancer type. Survival varies widely among different tumor types. For example, 5-year relative survival for glioblastoma is 5.7%, but for astrocytoma, the most common type of glioma, survival is 43.6%.\"\n\nSo brain cancer overall has a survival rate that varies widely. I should probably use a more specific number.\n\nLet me take a step back and reconsider the entire approach.\n\nActually, I think the most reasonable interpretation is:\n1. \"Most deadly cancers\" = cancers that cause the most deaths (total deaths)\n2. User wants survival rates in brackets to show how lethal each is\n\nSo the 5 most deadly cancers by total deaths (excluding pancreatic) are:\n1. Lung cancer - 124,730 deaths, 28% survival\n2. Colorectal cancer - 52,900 deaths, ~65% survival\n3. Breast cancer (female) - 42,170 deaths, ~91% survival\n4. Prostate cancer - 35,770 deaths, ~97% survival\n5. Liver cancer - 30,090 deaths, 22% survival\n\nBut this doesn't feel right because breast and prostate have very high survival rates. The word \"deadly\" suggests lethality, not just total deaths.\n\nLet me try another approach: I'll interpret \"most deadly\" as cancers with low survival rates that also cause significant deaths. This gives:\n\n1. Lung cancer - 28% survival, 124,730 deaths\n2. Liver cancer - 22% survival, 30,090 deaths\n3. Esophageal cancer - 22% survival, ~16,000 deaths (estimated)\n4. Brain cancer - ~36% survival, ~18,000 deaths (estimated)\n5. Stomach cancer - 37.9% survival, 10,780 deaths\n\nActually, I realize I need to search for the exact survival rates and death numbers for esophageal and brain cancers.\n\nLet me also consider that the user might want cancers with the lowest survival rates, regardless of total deaths. In that case:\n1. Glioblastoma - 5-7% survival\n2. Mesothelioma - 12% survival\n3. Liver cancer - 22% survival\n4. 